# Routes MCP tool work to its skill.
#
# A tool reaches agents through a descriptor row AND a handler, and the two have
# separate failure modes: a handler that needs a panel cannot be exposed headlessly,
# a "read" that writes is a lie the agent surface cannot detect, and every tool must
# declare its credential need or the build fails on a missing ledger row.
rule_id=mcp-tool
rule_once=1

rule_match() {
    case "$1" in
        Edit|Write) ;;
        *) return 1 ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$3" ] || return 1
    case "$3" in
        */features/ai/server/*Descriptors.ts) return 0 ;;
        */mcp-server.ts) return 0 ;;
    esac
    return 1
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
You are changing the MCP tool surface. Invoke the `mcp-tool-authoring` skill
(Skill tool, skill: "mcp-tool-authoring") first.

It carries the three things a descriptor row cannot tell you: headless safety (a
handler that needs a panel cannot serve an agent), read-vs-action honesty (a tool
declared a read that writes is undetectable from the agent side), and the
credential declaration every tool must carry — `needsAuth` is REQUIRED, so a new
tool without it fails the build rather than failing a user.

Counts are pinned in several places on purpose; if a count test fails after your
change, the pin is usually right.

This hook fires only once per session.
MSG
}
